Therapist: Hey Theo, come on in. It's been two weeks since I saw you — how have things been?
Client: Busier than I expected, honestly. Work picked up right after our last session, and then everything with my dad's care got more complicated on top of it.
Therapist: Let's start with work, and then I definitely want to get into the caregiving piece, since that sounded like it was weighing on you last time. What's been going on there?
Client: We're in the middle of a big client migration at Castlebridge Analytics, and I ended up as the lead on it, which I didn't ask for, but nobody else really wanted it either.
Therapist: How has carrying that landed for you?
Client: Exhausting, mostly. I'm doing the actual technical work during the day and then answering client emails at night because the client's in a different time zone. I've been getting maybe six hours of sleep most nights.
Therapist: Has that six hours been consistent, or has it been worse some nights than others?
Client: Worse on Sundays and Mondays, for sure. I lie there running through everything that could go wrong with the migration on Tuesday. Once I'm actually in the work week it evens out a little.
Therapist: What is it about this particular migration that's carrying so much weight, compared to other projects you've led before?
Client: The client's one of our biggest accounts, and there's a VP on their side who's already made it clear in emails that they're watching this closely after a rough migration with a different vendor last year. So there's history there that isn't even about us, but it's landing on us anyway.
Therapist: So you're not just managing the technical risk, you're also managing someone else's leftover distrust from an unrelated prior experience.
Client: Right, and it feels like there's no room for even a small hiccup, because it'll get read as confirming their worst assumption.
Therapist: That makes sense — a lot of anticipatory anxiety tends to cluster right before the thing you're dreading. What does the actual falling-asleep process look like on one of those Sunday nights?
Client: I get into bed around eleven, and then it's just this loop of technical scenarios — what if the data migration script times out, what if the client's IT team isn't actually ready on their end like they said. I'll check my phone a few times even though I know there's nothing new to see.
Therapist: How long would you say that loop typically runs before you actually fall asleep?
Client: An hour, sometimes more. Last Sunday it was probably ninety minutes.
Therapist: That's a significant chunk of time lost to rehearsing worst-case scenarios your body can't actually do anything about at eleven at night. How's your appetite been through all this?
Client: About the same, actually. I've noticed I'm not really tasting food some days, just eating because it's time to eat, but I haven't lost weight or anything.
Therapist: Good to know that one's held steady. Any caffeine or alcohol changes worth mentioning?
Client: More coffee than usual, probably. I've gone from two cups to something like four most days, just to get through the afternoons.
Therapist: That's worth flagging alongside the sleep piece — four cups can absolutely be contributing to how hard it is to wind down at night, on top of the anxiety itself. We might come back to that. Before we get to your dad, how has all of this been landing at home, with Sam?
Client: Not amazing, if I'm being honest. We used to cook dinner together most nights, and lately I'm either still on a client call or too fried to really be present for it.
Therapist: How has Sam been responding to that shift?
Client: Patient, mostly, which almost makes it worse somehow. Last week he said, half joking, "I miss having a boyfriend who isn't also my roommate," and I laughed it off, but it landed.
Therapist: What do you think he was getting at underneath the joke?
Client: That we're technically in the same apartment every night, but we're not really connecting. I'm on my laptop, he's doing his own thing, and we might exchange twenty words before bed.
Therapist: How long would you say that's been the pattern?
Client: Probably since the migration kicked off, so, six or seven weeks now.
Therapist: Has he said anything about wanting something specific to change, or was it more just naming what he's noticed?
Client: Mostly just naming it. He's not big on ultimatums. But I could tell it wasn't purely a joke.
Therapist: I want to hold onto that, because it connects to some of what we're going to talk about with your painting and your own values later today. How has it felt to you, this drop in connection with him?
Client: Guilty, honestly. He's been genuinely supportive about the caregiving stuff, and I feel like I'm not holding up my end of things right now.
Therapist: That's worth us coming back to when we get to the values piece, since protecting time with Sam might belong right alongside the painting as something worth actively defending on the calendar, not just hoping it happens. Before we move on, how has your body been holding up through all of this? Any tension, headaches, that kind of thing?
Client: Actually, yeah, now that you mention it. I've been getting these tension headaches most afternoons, right around my temples, and I catch myself clenching my jaw at my desk sometimes.
Therapist: How long has the jaw clenching been happening?
Client: Probably as long as the migration's been going, so, six or seven weeks, same as everything else we've been talking about.
Therapist: Have you noticed anything that makes the headaches better or worse specifically?
Client: Worse definitely on days with back-to-back client calls. Better on the weekend, though even then it's not totally gone.
Therapist: That pattern — worse with sustained tension, easing but not disappearing on rest days — fits pretty cleanly with stress-related muscle tension rather than anything else. Would you be open to trying a brief check-in with your jaw and shoulders a few times a day, just noticing if they're clenched and consciously releasing them?
Client: I can try that. I don't even usually notice I'm doing it until it's already been tight for a while.
Therapist: Setting a couple of alarms, maybe once mid-morning and once mid-afternoon, just to pause and check in with your jaw and shoulders, could help catch it earlier before it builds into a full headache.
Client: That's easy enough to set up. I'll do late morning and mid-afternoon, around when the headaches usually start anyway.
Therapist: Now, you mentioned your dad's care getting more complicated — what changed?
Client: My sister Naomi and I have been splitting his appointments, and she called me last week saying she can't keep doing the Tuesday afternoons anymore because of her own kids' schedule. Which, fine, that's fair, but it means I'm now doing three appointments a week instead of one, and I'm still trying to run this migration.
Therapist: That's a real collision of two big responsibilities at once. How did that conversation with Naomi go, beyond just the logistics?
Client: Honestly, a little tense. I said okay, I'll take it, but I definitely said it in a tone that wasn't really okay. She apologized after, and I said don't worry about it, but I don't think either of us fully let it go.
Therapist: It sounds like there's some unspoken resentment sitting under the surface of that exchange, on both sides maybe.
Client: Yeah. I love my dad, and I don't resent taking care of him, but I do feel like I'm the one who always ends up saying yes, and she knows that about me and sort of leans on it.
Therapist: Has that pattern — you saying yes, her leaning on it — been true for a long time, or is this something newer since your dad's diagnosis?
Client: Honestly, probably my whole life. Growing up, I was the one who'd cover for her if she missed curfew, cover shifts at our parents' store, that kind of thing. It just got more visible once there was something this serious to actually split.
Therapist: That's worth us coming back to later today, the pattern of you being the default yes going back that far. I want to ask something more specific about your dad first — how has he been doing medically since we last talked?
Client: About the same. The Parkinson's is progressing slowly, his neurologist doesn't think there's been a big change, but the day-to-day stuff, getting him to appointments, managing his medication schedule, that part's gotten heavier just because there's more of it.
Therapist: Can you walk me through what a typical appointment day actually looks like for you logistically?
Client: I usually leave work around one, pick him up by one-thirty, we're at the office by two, and depending on how backed up they are, I'm not dropping him back home until four or four-thirty. Then I'm making up the work hours that night.
Therapist: So each appointment day is effectively costing you an entire afternoon plus evening work time to compensate.
Client: When you say it like that, yeah, it's basically a half day gone, three times a week now instead of once.
Therapist: Has anything happened recently that made the caregiving feel more urgent, beyond just the added frequency?
Client: Actually, yeah. Two weeks ago he had a fall in the bathroom, nothing broken, just some bruising on his hip, but I got the call at work and had to leave the migration planning meeting to go check on him.
Therapist: That sounds like it would have been frightening in the moment, separate from how it turned out.
Client: It was. I remember driving over there thinking a dozen different worst-case things before I even knew how bad it was.
Therapist: How was he, once you actually saw him?
Client: Shaken up more than hurt, honestly. He kept apologizing for making me leave work, which almost made it worse.
Therapist: Has anything changed since then, in terms of precautions at his place?
Client: We put in a grab bar by the shower, and Naomi and I agreed one of us should check in by phone every evening now, just to be safe.
Therapist: That sounds like a reasonable adjustment, though I imagine it's one more thing added to an already full plate for both of you.
Client: It is, but it felt necessary after that. I'd rather have the extra check-in than another scare like that.
Therapist: That kind of near-miss can also linger even after the immediate danger passes — it makes sense if some of this week's heaviness traces back to that as much as to the diagnosis anniversary.
Client: I hadn't really connected those two things either, but yeah, they were close together, the fall and the anniversary.
Therapist: That kind of slow, incremental increase in caregiving load is its own particular kind of exhausting, because there's no single moment where it suddenly became too much — it just accumulates.
Client: That's exactly it. Nothing dramatic happened, it's just more.
Therapist: I want to check in on something we've talked about before, since it's been a few months — how have you been doing with your own mood, day to day, separate from the stress of work and caregiving?
Client: Honestly, not great the last week or so. Heavier than usual. Flatter, I guess is the word.
Therapist: Can you say more about what flatter looks like for you?
Client: Just less interested in things I normally like. I used to look forward to painting on weekends, and the last two Saturdays I just didn't feel like it. Sat on the couch instead.
Therapist: When you sat on the couch instead, what was actually going through your mind, if you remember?
Client: Mostly just a kind of "what's the point" feeling. Not in a scary way, just like nothing felt worth the effort of setting up paints.
Therapist: How does that compare to where you were six months ago, when we started adjusting your sertraline with Dr. Whitcombe?
Client: Better than before the adjustment, for sure. This doesn't feel as bad as it did back then. But it's definitely worse than a month ago.
Therapist: Is there anything you can point to that shifted around when this current stretch started?
Client: Maybe the anniversary of my dad's diagnosis was a couple weeks ago. I didn't think much of it at the time, but looking back, that's roughly when things started feeling heavier.
Therapist: Anniversary reactions can show up even when you don't consciously register the date as significant in the moment — your system can register it before your conscious mind catches up.
Client: That actually tracks. I hadn't put those two things together until just now, saying it out loud.
Therapist: I want to go back to that day, if you're willing, since it sounds like it might still be carrying some weight. What was the actual day of his diagnosis like for you, looking back?
Client: I remember the neurologist's office had this weirdly bright lighting, and my dad just went completely quiet after she said the word Parkinson's. He didn't ask a single question. I had to ask all of them.
Therapist: That must have put you in a strange position, having to advocate and process at the same time.
Client: Yeah. Sorry — give me a second. Talking about that appointment still gets me a little more than I expect it to.
Therapist: Take whatever time you need. There's nothing to apologize for.
Client: I think what gets me is that in that moment, in that office, I remember thinking, okay, I guess this is my job now. Like I decided it right there without really deciding it.
Therapist: That's a significant thing to have carried alone in that moment. I notice you're tearing up telling me this.
Client: Yeah. It's still close to the surface, even a year and a half out.
Therapist: I want to check in with you right now, since this clearly brought up a lot. Would it help to do a brief grounding exercise together before we keep going?
Client: Yeah, actually, that might help. My chest feels a little tight.
Therapist: Let's do the five senses. Can you name five things you can see in this room right now?
Client: The plant in the corner, your bookshelf, the tissue box, my water bottle, and that clock on the wall.
Therapist: Good. Now four things you can physically feel.
Client: The chair under me, my feet on the floor, my jacket sleeve, and my hands resting in my lap.
Therapist: Three things you can hear.
Client: The fan in the corner, some traffic outside, and your voice.
Therapist: Two things you can smell.
Client: Maybe the coffee I brought in with me, and just the room in general.
Therapist: And one thing you can taste.
Client: Still a little of that coffee, honestly.
Therapist: How's your chest feeling now compared to a minute ago?
Client: Better. Not completely gone, but the tightness eased up some.
Therapist: That grounding technique engages your senses in the present moment, which helps interrupt the loop your mind was caught in. Grief around a parent's diagnosis doesn't always look like grief over a death — it can be grief over the version of the relationship, or the future, you thought you were going to have.
Client: I hadn't thought about it that way, but yeah, that's probably part of it.
Therapist: I want to ask you something I ask most people going through a hard stretch like this, and it's not because of anything specific you've said — has any of this made you think about hurting yourself, or that you'd be better off not being here?
Client: No, nothing like that. I get tired and frustrated, but I've never had thoughts like that, even at the hardest points with my dad.
Therapist: I appreciate you answering that directly. And just to check the other direction — is there anyone or anything currently making you feel unsafe, at home or otherwise?
Client: No, nothing like that either. It's really just exhaustion and low mood, not anything scarier than that.
Therapist: Good, that's important for me to know, and I'll keep checking in periodically the way I do with everyone. Let's shift back to the work stress for a bit, since I think there's something useful we can do with the specific thought pattern you mentioned earlier. You said you lie awake running through everything that could go wrong with the migration — can you walk me through what that actually sounds like in your head?
Client: It's usually something like, if this migration fails, the client's going to pull the account, and then it's going to be obviously my fault since I'm the lead, and I'll probably get pushed out.
Therapist: Let's slow that down using the thought record we've used before. What's the evidence that supports the idea that a failed migration would be entirely your fault and would get you pushed out?
Client: I mean, I am the lead, so if it fails, some of that is going to land on me. That's just how it works.
Therapist: Is there anything beyond "I'm the lead" supporting the idea that it would be entirely your fault, or that it would specifically get you pushed out?
Client: Not really, when I think about it. There've been other projects that hit snags and nobody got fired over it.
Therapist: What's the evidence against the thought — anything from how this project or past ones have actually gone?
Client: My manager's actually been pretty supportive so far, checking in without micromanaging. And the two engineers under me on this know what they're doing. It's not just riding on me alone.
Therapist: So holding both sides together, what's a more balanced version of that 2am thought?
Client: Maybe something like, this project has real risk in it, but I'm not carrying it by myself, and one rough migration wouldn't erase everything else I've done there.
Therapist: How does that version sit with you compared to the original?
Client: A lot less like a punch to the chest. Still nervous about the migration itself, just not nervous about my whole job hanging on it.
Therapist: That's exactly the shift we're going for — not eliminating the concern, just right-sizing it. Let's actually run through a second example, since repeating the process helps it become more automatic. Was there another moment recently where a similar spiral happened?
Client: Yeah, actually. Yesterday one of the junior engineers flagged that a test suite was failing, and my first thought was, I clearly haven't trained them well enough and this whole migration is going to be a mess because of gaps in my leadership.
Therapist: Same process — what's the evidence for that thought?
Client: One test suite failed. That's really the only concrete thing.
Therapist: And against it?
Client: Turns out it wasn't even related to anything I trained them on — it was a flaky test that's been unreliable for months, way before this project started.
Therapist: So what's the more balanced thought, looking at both sides?
Client: Something like, one flaky test failed for reasons that have nothing to do with my training, and one data point doesn't mean my leadership on this project is failing.
Therapist: How quickly did you get to that balanced version compared to the migration example a minute ago?
Client: Actually pretty fast. I could see the pattern the second time through — jumping straight from one bad data point to a sweeping conclusion about myself.
Therapist: That's exactly the kind of skill-building we're going for — each time you deliberately walk through it, the next one gets faster and more automatic. I'd like you to try writing the balanced version down somewhere you'll actually see it, maybe a sticky note on your monitor, for the week leading up to the migration.
Client: I can do that. I've got a notepad right next to my keyboard already, so that's easy.
Therapist: Good. Now I want to come back to the pattern with Naomi, the being the default yes, since it sounds like it goes back to childhood. What do you think keeps you saying yes even now, as an adult, when it costs you like this?
Client: I guess I feel like if I say no, either the appointment doesn't happen, or it falls entirely on her, and then I feel guilty either way.
Therapist: What would it actually look like, concretely, to say no to one of the three appointments this week?
Client: I guess I could tell her I can only reliably do two right now because of the migration, and we need to figure out the third together instead of it defaulting to me.
Therapist: How does saying that sentence out loud feel, right now, separate from whether you'd actually say it to her?
Client: Uncomfortable. Kind of like I'm letting everyone down just by saying it.
Therapist: Let's actually practice saying it, with me playing Naomi for a moment, so it's a little less foreign when the real conversation happens.
Client: Okay, that feels a little strange, but sure.
Therapist: Alright — Theo, I really need you to take Tuesday again this week, I just can't make it work.
Client: I can do two appointments reliably this week because of the migration at work, but the third one, we need to figure out together instead of it just becoming mine by default.
Therapist: How did it feel to actually say that, even in a practice version?
Client: Weirdly harder than I expected, even knowing it wasn't the real conversation. My instinct is so strongly just to say yes and figure it out later.
Therapist: That instinct makes sense given how long you've operated that way, but you can see how it connects to the exhaustion we've been talking about all session. What specifically makes it feel so uncomfortable, if you slow down and look at it?
Client: I think I'm scared she'll feel like I'm abandoning her, or like I think I do more than her, even though that's not really what I mean.
Therapist: So part of the discomfort is worrying about how the boundary will land for her, not just the discomfort of setting it.
Client: Yeah, exactly. I don't want her to feel attacked, I just genuinely can't keep doing three appointments and the migration at the same time.
Therapist: Is there a way to say that part explicitly, the part about not wanting her to feel attacked, when you actually have the conversation?
Client: Maybe something like, this isn't about what you're doing wrong, it's that I physically can't sustain three a week on top of work right now.
Therapist: That addition does a lot of work — it names the limit as coming from your capacity, not as a judgment of her.
Client: That actually makes me feel a little better about bringing it up. It's less like I'm accusing her of not doing enough.
Therapist: I'd like you to actually use a version of that fuller line for real this week.
Client: I'll try. It'll probably feel just as awkward in the moment as it did just now, but I think having the extra sentence ready will help.
Therapist: That's completely normal the first few times — the goal isn't for it to feel comfortable yet, just for you to actually say it. I want to check in on the painting, too, since you mentioned skipping it the last two weekends. What do you think has been getting in the way?
Client: Honestly, by the time Saturday comes around I just feel drained, and painting takes some setup, so it's easier to just not.
Therapist: Would you be open to lowering the bar for what counts, just for this week? Not a full painting session, just getting the materials out for ten minutes?
Client: That actually sounds doable. I think the all-or-nothing version of it is part of what's stopping me.
Therapist: That's a really common pattern with things that used to bring us pleasure — the standard creeps up over time until it feels like too much to start. Ten minutes, materials out, no expectation of finishing anything.
Client: I can commit to that. Saturday morning, before anything else takes over the day.
Therapist: I want to circle back to what you said earlier about Sam, since it fits the same pattern — something that matters to you getting quietly squeezed out by everything else. What would one small, protected piece of connection time with him look like this week?
Client: Maybe just committing to actually cooking dinner together on Thursday, no laptop at the table, even if I have to close it out mid-thought on something for work.
Therapist: How realistic does Thursday specifically feel, given the migration timeline?
Client: Thursday's actually before the heaviest crunch, which is more like Monday and Tuesday of the following week. So it should hold.
Therapist: I'd like you to protect that one, the same way we're protecting the ten minutes of painting — treat it as something on the calendar, not something that happens if there's time left over.
Client: I can tell Sam tonight that I want to do that, actually. I think just naming it out loud to him will help me follow through.
Therapist: That's a great instinct — saying it to him turns it into a shared commitment instead of just a private intention you can quietly let slide. While we're on the subject of things that have quietly dropped off — you used to mention running a few times a week. Is that still happening at all?
Client: Not in probably two months. Same story as the painting, really — by the end of the day I don't have it in me, and mornings go straight to emails before my dad's appointments.
Therapist: What did running used to do for you, when you were still doing it regularly?
Client: It was honestly the one time my brain would go quiet. No migration thoughts, no caregiving logistics, just the route and my breathing.
Therapist: That sounds like it was functioning as a real release valve, separate from anything social or productive — just quiet.
Client: Yeah, I miss that specifically. Not even the fitness part, just the quiet.
Therapist: Given everything else we're already adding this week — the painting, the Sam dinner, the conversation with Naomi — I don't want to overload you, but is there a small version of running that could fit without becoming one more obligation?
Client: Maybe just one short run, even fifteen minutes, on a day I'm not doing a dad appointment. Wednesday could work.
Therapist: That sounds appropriately sized given how much else is already on the list this week. One fifteen-minute run on Wednesday, no pressure beyond that.
Client: I can try for that. Low bar, like the painting.
Therapist: I want to touch on the sleep and coffee piece again before we move on, since it connects to a lot of what we've covered. Would you be willing to try capping coffee at two cups, and only before noon, for the next week?
Client: I think I can do two. Cutting the afternoon ones specifically makes sense, since those are probably what's keeping me wired at eleven.
Therapist: And on the nights the anticipatory loop starts, would you be willing to try writing the specific worry down on paper instead of letting it run in your head, even just one line?
Client: I could keep a notepad on the nightstand. That seems more manageable than trying to just will myself to stop thinking about it.
Therapist: That's a good pairing with the thought record work we did today — getting the worry out of your head and onto paper where you can actually look at it instead of just cycling through it. I also want to loop back to the medication conversation, since it sounds like this stretch has been harder than the last month. Have you had any contact with Dr. Whitcombe recently?
Client: Not since the last adjustment, about three months ago now. I've just been on the same 100 milligrams since then.
Therapist: How has that dose been treating you overall, side-effect-wise, separate from this particular rough stretch?
Client: Pretty well, actually. I had some nausea the first couple weeks after the increase, but that settled down. No real complaints since.
Therapist: Good to know the dose itself has been well tolerated. Given this recent dip, tied to what sounds like an anniversary reaction plus a real increase in external stressors, I think it's worth you touching base with her, not necessarily to change anything, but so she has current information.
Client: That's fair. I've been putting off calling because I felt like I should be able to handle a rough couple weeks without looping her back in, but I hear what you're saying.
Therapist: You don't need to wait until things are unmanageable to loop in your prescriber — that's exactly what check-ins are for. I'd also like to make a referral alongside that, if you're open to it — there's a caregiver support group that meets through the hospital system, specifically for adult children managing a parent's chronic illness. I think being in a room with other people carrying a similar load could help with some of what we talked about today, especially the default-yes pattern with Naomi.
Client: I think I'd actually be interested in that. I don't really talk to anyone else going through the same thing.
Therapist: I noticed a little hesitation before you said that — is there anything about the idea of a group specifically that gives you pause?
Client: Honestly, I've never been someone who does support groups. I always pictured it as sitting in a circle sharing feelings with strangers, which isn't really my thing.
Therapist: That's a really common picture people have, and it's worth saying that a lot of these groups are more practical than that — swapping actual strategies for scheduling, respite, navigating a parent's care team, alongside the emotional piece, not just an open feelings circle.
Client: That version sounds more manageable, honestly. I could see myself getting something out of that.
Therapist: You're also under no obligation to keep going if the first one or two sessions aren't a fit — think of it as trying it on rather than committing to it indefinitely.
Client: That helps. I think I was picturing it as an all-or-nothing thing, same as the painting, honestly.
Therapist: That's a good catch, and probably worth noticing as a pattern of its own — a lot of things in your life seem to get framed as all-or-nothing before you've even tried the small version.
Client: Yeah, I'm noticing that theme a lot today, actually.
Therapist: I'll send that referral over today — it's run by a clinical social worker named Priya Denholm, and the group meets Wednesday evenings. Her office will likely reach out directly to schedule your intake.
Client: Okay, I'll keep an eye out for that call.
Therapist: Before we wrap up, I want to run through a couple of administrative things, if that's all right.
Client: Sure, go ahead.
Therapist: I want to confirm your contact information is still current for our records. Phone number still the one ending in 0133?
Client: Yes, 555-0133 is right, and the email's still theo.b1985@fakemail.example.
Therapist: Perfect, matches what we have. And your address on file — still 47 Larkspur Court, apartment 12?
Client: Yes, still there.
Therapist: Good, and I see your insurance renewed under the same Cobalt Health PPO plan this year — any change in authorization on your end?
Client: No, same plan, and HR at Castlebridge confirmed the mental health benefit didn't change for this plan year, so we should be fine continuing weekly.
Therapist: Great, one less thing to worry about. And just to double check, is Naomi still listed as your emergency contact, or would you want that updated to Sam at this point?
Client: Actually, let's update it to Sam. Naomi's already stretched thin with her own kids, and Sam's usually the one who'd actually be reachable fastest anyway.
Therapist: I'll make that change — Sam as primary emergency contact going forward. Do you have a good number for him on file, or should we use yours as the point of contact for now?
Client: You can use my number for now, and I'll get you his direct line by our next session if you need it separately.
Therapist: That works for the time being. So, to summarize today — we talked through the added caregiving load with your dad and the tension with Naomi going back to childhood, we talked about the drop in connection with Sam and you're protecting Thursday dinner together with no laptop, we did a grounding exercise when the memory of his diagnosis day came up strongly, we worked through two thought records on the migration and training anxieties and landed on more balanced versions of both to write down this week, you're going to have the conversation with Naomi about redistributing the third appointment, you're capping coffee at two cups before noon and writing down nighttime worries instead of looping on them, you're committing to ten minutes of painting setup on Saturday, you'll reach out to Dr. Whitcombe to update her given this dip, and I'm sending the referral to the caregiver support group with Priya Denholm.
Client: That's a lot, but it all actually makes sense together, hearing it laid out like that.
Therapist: I want to name something before we close — you did real work today, especially going back into the day of your dad's diagnosis and staying with it instead of brushing past it. That's not a small thing to revisit.
Client: Thank you. I think I needed to actually say some of that out loud instead of just carrying it around.
Therapist: I'm glad you did. Let's keep our regular time next week, and we'll start with how the conversation with Naomi and the call to Dr. Whitcombe both went.
Client: Sounds good. Thanks, really.
Therapist: Take care of yourself this week, Theo. I'll see you then.
